We have a C function `parse_csv_line` in `/repo/src/csv_parser.c` that reads a comma-separated line into a fixed-size buffer. The function is causing a buffer overrun when the input line exactly fills the buffer. Here's the relevant code:

```c
void parse_csv_line(const char *input, char *output, size_t out_size) {
    size_t i = 0, j = 0;
    while (input[i] != '\0' && j < out_size) {
        if (input[i] == ',') {
            output[j] = '\0';
            return;
        }
        output[j++] = input[i++];
    }
    output[j] = '\0';  // Potential off-by-one: j can equal out_size here
}
```

If the input line has exactly `out_size-1` characters before a comma, the loop writes characters up to index `out_size-2`, then after the loop `output[j]` writes at index `out_size-1` which is fine, but if there is no comma and the input length is exactly `out_size-1`, then after the loop `j == out_size-1` and `output[j]` writes at the last valid index, but if the input length is `out_size`, then the loop condition `j < out_size` fails when `j == out_size-1` (since `out_size-1 < out_size` is true) and we write `output[out_size-1]` then `j` becomes `out_size`, then loop exits and writes `output[out_size]` which is out of bounds.

